{"content":"🟡 **IMPORTANT: The new comment misstates the race mechanism — it contradicts the accurate comment directly above it**

The added explanation says release can lag *"the awaited close()"*, but in this exact path the test's `await timedOut.close()` does **not** await the socket close:

```dart
// openrouter_oauth_callback.dart
_timeoutTimer = Timer(timeout, () {
  ...
  unawaited(close());   // ← nulls _server, then awaits server.close() in flight
});

Future<void> close() async {
  _timeoutTimer?.cancel();
  final server = _server;
  _server = null;
  if (server != null) await server.close();  // ← skipped on re-entry: _server is already null
}
```

When the timer fires, `unawaited(close())` runs the synchronous part (cancels the timer, nulls `_server`) and leaves `server.close()` in flight. The test's subsequent `await timedOut.close()` therefore returns immediately without touching the socket — which is precisely why the retry is needed. The pre-existing comment right above already explains this correctly ("the timeout closes the socket asynchronously (`unawaited(close())` in the timer callback)"); the new text now gives a conflicting explanation on the same ten lines.

A future maintainer debugging the next flake will read "lag the awaited close()", conclude an awaited close was insufficient, and go looking for an OS-level bug that isn't there. Suggested wording:

```dart
// The port is released: a bind on the same port succeeds. The timer's
// unawaited(close()) leaves server.close() in flight (a re-entered
// timedOut.close() is a no-op), so the bind can race the still-closing
// socket on loaded CI runners ("the shared flag" error) — prove release
// with a bounded retry instead of asserting instant release.
```

No behavior change requested — the retry itself is the right fix.
